| Premise AD:DH = 2/1 gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD²: 1, DH²: -1}
| Premise CD:DG = 2/1 gives a linear equation with coefficients {CD²: 1, DG²: -1}
| Then {AD²: 1, DH²: -1} + -1 x {CD²: 1, DG²: -1}
|    = {AD²: 1, DH²: -1, CD²: -1, DG²: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AD²: 1, DH²: -1, CD²: -1, DG²: 1} that gives AD:CD = DH:DG



017. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(AB,BC) = ∠(BE,BF):
| Premise AB ∥ AE gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB: 1, AE: -1}
| Premise AE ∥ BE gives a linear equation with coefficients {AE: 1, BE: -1}
| Then {AB: -1, AE: 1} + -1 x {AE: 1, BE: -1}
|    = {AB: -1, BE: 1}
| Premise BF ∥ BC gives a linear equation with coefficients {BC: 1, BF: -1}
| Then {AB: -1, BE: 1} + 1 x {BC: 1, BF: -1}
|    = {AB: -1, BE: 1, BC: 1, BF: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AB: -1, BE: 1, BC: 1, BF: -1} that gives ∠(AB,BC) = ∠(BE,BF)



020. | Ratio Chasing to prove AB:BC = BE:BF:
| Premise AB:BE = 2/1 gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB²: 1, BE²: -1}
| Premise BC:BF = 2/1 gives a linear equation with coefficients {BC²: 1, BF²: -1}
| Then {AB²: 1, BE²: -1} + -1 x {BC²: 1, BF²: -1}
|    = {AB²: 1, BE²: -1, BC²: -1, BF²: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AB²: 1, BE²: -1, BC²: -1, BF²: 1} that gives AB:BC = BE:BF



023. | Angle Chasing to prove EF ∥ GH:
| Premise ∠(AC,AD) = ∠(GH,DH) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: -1, AD: 1, DH: -1, GH: 1}
| Premise ∠(AC,BC) = ∠(EF,BF) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: -1, BC: 1, BF: -1, EF: 1}
| Then {AC: 1, AD: -1, DH: 1, GH: -1} + 1 x {AC: -1, BC: 1, BF: -1, EF: 1}
|    = {AD: -1, DH: 1, GH: -1, BC: 1, BF: -1, EF: 1}
| Premise AD ∥ AH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD: 1, AH: -1}
| Then {AD: -1, DH: 1, GH: -1, BC: 1, BF: -1, EF: 1} + 1 x {AD: 1, AH: -1}
|    = {DH: 1, GH: -1, BC: 1, BF: -1, EF: 1, AH: -1}
| Premise BF ∥ BC gives a linear equation with coefficients {BC: 1, BF: -1}
| Then {DH: 1, GH: -1, BC: 1, BF: -1, EF: 1, AH: -1} + -1 x {BC: 1, BF: -1}
|    = {DH: 1, GH: -1, EF: 1, AH: -1}
| Premise DH ∥ AH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AH: 1, DH: -1}
| Then {DH: 1, GH: -1, EF: 1, AH: -1} + 1 x {AH: 1, DH: -1}
|    = {GH: -1, EF: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {GH: -1, EF: 1} that gives EF ∥ GH

